  <section class="hero" aria-labelledby="hero-title">
    <div class="hero-copy">
      <p class="kicker"><code>@ghostpaw/telegram</code> <span class="ver">0.7.6</span></p>
      <h1 id="hero-title">Your bot remembers the chat, including what was edited.</h1>
      <p class="lede">telegram writes every incoming update into a SQLite file before your handler runs: messages, edits, replies, reactions, members and files. Your bot and your agent read from that record, and the agent's tools return plans until you switch execution on.</p>
      <div class="install">
        <p class="cmd-label" id="lib-label">Binary for linux-x64 (checked against SHA256SUMS)</p>
        <div class="cmd"><input id="cmd-lib" readonly value="curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/GhostPawJS/telegram/main/install.sh | sh -s -- --target linux-x64 &amp;&amp; export PATH=&quot;$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH&quot;" aria-labelledby="lib-label" spellcheck="false"><button type="button" class="copy" data-copy="cmd-lib">Copy</button></div>
        <p class="cmd-label" id="mcp-label">Then run it as a local MCP server</p>
        <div class="cmd"><input id="cmd-mcp" readonly value="ghostpaw-telegram mcp" aria-labelledby="mcp-label" spellcheck="false"><button type="button" class="copy" data-copy="cmd-mcp">Copy</button></div>
        <p class="cmd-label"><a href="#binaries">macOS, Windows, pinned versions, npx and the npm library</a></p>
      </div>
      <p class="actions"><a class="button" href="#run">Replay the run</a> <a href="{{.urls.github}}">Read the source on GitHub</a></p>
      <p class="terms">Free and MIT licensed; the binary carries its own Node.js and the npm package needs Node.js 24 or newer, with zero runtime dependencies. The mirror is a SQLite file on your disk; the bot token stays in your environment.</p>
    </div>
    <figure class="hero-art frame">
      <div class="scope"><picture><source media="(min-width: 52rem)" srcset="assets/images/hero-720.webp" width="720" height="540"><img src="assets/images/hero-480.webp" width="480" height="360" alt="A wireframe paper plane drawn in cyan light, a violet dotted orbit looping from it into a stacked database cylinder with a glowing paw on its lid." fetchpriority="high"></picture><span class="scan" aria-hidden="true"></span></div>
      <p class="readout" aria-label="Zero runtime dependencies"><span class="big">0</span><span>runtime dependencies</span></p>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section id="run" class="sec run-sec" aria-labelledby="run-title">
    <h2 id="run-title">One group, three updates, six calls</h2>
    <p class="intro">Your handler never has to save anything. The row is already there.</p>
    <p class="intro">This ran with <code>createBot</code> and long polling against telegram 0.6.2 on 2026-10-24. The Telegram servers were replaced by a local stand-in passed through the documented <code>api.fetch</code> option, so three updates arrive in a group called Renewal desk and nothing reaches Telegram. Everything else is the library. Run the steps in order.</p>
    <div class="run">
      <div class="state" role="region" aria-labelledby="state-title" aria-live="polite">
        <p class="state-label" id="state-title">Mirror</p>
        <ul id="state-list">
          <li><span class="k">Messages stored</span><span class="v">0</span></li>
          <li><span class="k">Edits kept</span><span class="v">0</span></li>
          <li><span class="k">Outbound calls</span><span class="v">0</span></li>
          <li><span class="k">Last read</span><span class="v">—</span></li>
          <li><span class="k">Tool result</span><span class="v">—</span></li>
        </ul>
        <p class="state-foot" id="state-foot">Eight empty tables after initTelegramTables(db).</p>
      </div>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li class="step" id="step-1">
          <h3><span class="n">01</span> Lena posts in the group</h3>
          <p>The first update arrives through long polling.</p>
          <pre class="call"><code>const bot = createBot({ token, db, api: { fetch: standIn },
  onMessage: async ({ message }) =&gt; {
    read.getMessage(db, message.chatId, message.messageId); // already there?
  },
});
await bot.start();</code></pre>
          <div class="result"><pre><code>// update 1, chat -1001234 "Renewal desk", from Lena (@lena_ops)
// "Invoice 4471 is paid. The café receipt is in the shared drive."
// inside onMessage: read.getMessage(...) -&gt; row found
//   { messageId: 10, direction: 'in', fromDisplayName: 'Lena', type: 'text', … }</code></pre><p class="say">The row was written before the handler ran, so the handler can rely on it.</p></div>
          <button type="button" class="act run-btn">Run step 1</button>
        </li>
        <li class="step" id="step-2">
          <h3><span class="n">02</span> The handler replies</h3>
          <p>One plain-text reply from inside onMessage.</p>
          <pre class="call"><code>await write.sendMessage(bot.client, -1001234, 'Noted. Filed under 4471.');</code></pre>
          <div class="result"><pre><code>// the stand-in received:
// POST sendMessage { chat_id: -1001234, text: 'Noted. Filed under 4471.' }</code></pre><p class="say">One outbound call. With a real token this goes to api.telegram.org.</p></div>
          <button type="button" class="act run-btn">Run step 2</button>
        </li>
        <li class="step" id="step-3">
          <h3><span class="n">03</span> Lena edits her message</h3>
          <p>An <code>edited_message</code> update, one minute later.</p>
          <pre class="call"><code>// update 2: edited_message, msg 10
read.getMessage(db, -1001234, 10).text;
read.editHistory(db, -1001234, 10);</code></pre>
          <div class="result"><pre><code>// "Invoice 4471 is paid. The café receipt is in the finance folder."
// [{ id: 1, messageId: 10,
//    text: 'Invoice 4471 is paid. The café receipt is in the shared drive.' }]</code></pre><p class="say">The message shows the new text, and the old one is kept. You can see what changed and when.</p></div>
          <button type="button" class="act run-btn">Run step 3</button>
        </li>
        <li class="step" id="step-4">
          <h3><span class="n">04</span> Jun replies to it</h3>
          <p>A reply to message 10.</p>
          <pre class="call"><code>// update 3: "Which café?", reply_to_message: 10
read.replyChain(db, -1001234, 11);</code></pre>
          <div class="result"><pre><code>// [{ messageId: 10, text: 'Invoice 4471 is paid. The café receipt is in the finance folder.' },
//  { messageId: 11, text: 'Which café?' }]</code></pre><p class="say">The chain runs from the original down to the reply.</p></div>
          <button type="button" class="act run-btn">Run step 4</button>
        </li>
        <li class="step" id="step-5">
          <h3><span class="n">05</span> Search without the accent</h3>
          <p>FTS5 over the mirror, no network.</p>
          <pre class="call"><code>read.searchMessages(db, -1001234, 'cafe');</code></pre>
          <div class="result"><pre><code>// [{ messageId: 11, snippet: 'Which &lt;b&gt;café&lt;/b&gt;?' },
//  { messageId: 10, snippet: 'Invoice 4471 is paid. The &lt;b&gt;café&lt;/b&gt; receipt is in the finance folder.' }]</code></pre><p class="say">Both messages, accent or not. The snippet for message 10 shows the text after the edit.</p></div>
          <button type="button" class="act run-btn">Run step 5</button>
        </li>
        <li class="step" id="step-6">
          <h3><span class="n">06</span> The agent wants to answer</h3>
          <p>The <code>tg_send</code> tool, called the way an agent harness calls it.</p>
          <pre class="call"><code>tools.getTelegramToolByName('tg_send').handler(db, {
  subcommand: 'send_message', chatId: -1001234, text: 'Receipt found, thanks.',
});</code></pre>
          <div class="result"><pre><code>// { ok: true, outcome: 'success', summary: 'Message ready to send',
//   data: { action: 'send_message', chatId: -1001234, text: 'Receipt found, thanks.' } }</code></pre><p class="say">A plan, and the outbound count is still one: <code>getStats().messagesOut</code> reads 1. Sending takes <code>createTelegramToolFacade({ client, execute: true })</code>, which you have to write yourself.</p></div>
          <button type="button" class="act run-btn">Run step 6</button>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
    <div class="run-tools"><button type="button" class="button ghost" id="reset">Start over</button><p id="run-status" role="status"></p></div>
    <p class="note">In this run a search for “finance” found message 10 after the edit, and “shared” found nothing. Before 0.6.2 the index kept the old text.</p>
    <p class="note">The stand-in sent no files. With a real token, <code>createBot</code> downloads incoming files in the background; a failed download leaves the checksum empty so you can retry it.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="sec explain" aria-labelledby="explain-title">
    <div class="sec-head">
    <h2 id="explain-title">What the mirror keeps, and four tools to read it</h2>
    <img class="graph" src="assets/images/art-640.webp" width="640" height="360" alt="Cyan speech bubbles stacking down into a band of stored lines, with a violet magnifying glass over them." loading="lazy">
    </div>
    <p class="intro">Eight tables, created in one call. The agent reads them through four tools.</p>
    <dl class="form">
      <div><dt>Messages</dt><dd>Text, media, edits, reply edges, albums and threads, with an FTS5 index that folds diacritics.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Users, chats, members</dt><dd>Names, chat titles and types, member status, permissions and admin titles.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Reactions, callbacks</dt><dd>Emoji per user per message, and inline button presses with their data.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Files</dt><dd>Metadata, and the bytes as a BLOB, downloaded in the background and stored once per SHA-256 hash.</dd></div>
      <div><dt><code>tg_read</code></dt><dd>Messages, chats, users, reactions, edit history and reply chains.</dd></div>
      <div><dt><code>tg_send</code></dt><dd>Send, edit, delete, forward and pin, set reactions, broadcast.</dd></div>
      <div><dt><code>tg_manage</code></dt><dd>Ban, unban, restrict, promote and kick, and member status.</dd></div>
      <div><dt><code>tg_connect</code></dt><dd>Bot stats, bot state and active chats.</dd></div>
    </dl>
    <p class="note">Each tool carries its JSON Schema, a side-effect level and a <code>readOnly</code> flag, so a harness can tell reading from acting without reading docs.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="sec worry" aria-labelledby="worry-title">
    <h2 id="worry-title">If a bot that posts by itself worries you</h2>
    <div class="worry-grid">
      <p>Some people log every agent action to a Telegram bot as an audit trail. That trail is only useful if you know what the bot did on its own.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>The tools return plans by default. Execution needs an explicit facade with <code>execute: true</code>.</li>
        <li>The executing facade limits calls per chat and globally with token buckets, waits on Telegram's <code>retry_after</code> and retries twice by default. It masks bot tokens in errors.</li>
        <li>The MCP server executes sends only when <code>TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN</code> is set, keeps the mirror in <code>~/.ghostpaw/telegram.db</code> and does not poll for updates itself.</li>
        <li><code>createBot</code> takes a chat allowlist, <code>allowedChatIds</code>, and drops updates from other chats. The default empty list allows every chat, and it does not filter individual senders.</li>
        <li>Node.js 24 or newer, because it uses the built-in <code>node:sqlite</code> and global fetch.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec field" aria-labelledby="field-title">
    <h2 id="field-title">Where agent bots lose track</h2>
    <p class="intro">Bots that front an agent tend to fail in the same places. These are the ones telegram is built around.</p>
    <div class="field-rows">
      <article class="field-row"><h3>Group messages the bot saw but did not answer</h3><p class="gap">Most handlers drop them, so when the bot is finally mentioned it has no idea what came before.</p><p class="here">Every update Telegram delivers is stored in SQLite before your handler runs. In groups, privacy mode still limits what Telegram delivers.</p></article>
      <article class="field-row"><h3>Tools that act the moment they are called</h3><p class="gap">A wrong send or a wrong ban cannot be taken back.</p><p class="here">Send and moderation tools return a plan. They only act when <code>TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN</code> is set in the server environment.</p></article>
      <article class="field-row"><h3>Chat history held in a client's memory</h3><p class="gap">Long-running clients cache everything and have to be restarted when memory fills up.</p><p class="here">History lives on disk in SQLite, with edit history and reply chains, and files are stored once per SHA-256 hash.</p></article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec numbers" aria-labelledby="numbers-title">
    <h2 id="numbers-title">What it costs to add</h2>
    <dl class="ledger">
      <div><dt>Runtime dependencies</dt><dd>0</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Node.js</dt><dd>24 or newer (node:sqlite)</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Size of dist/index.js, gzip</dt><dd>34.2 KB</dd></div>
